      "q": "1. Discuss the salient features of the world distribution of precipitation.",
      "a": "wer:\nWorld distribution of precipitation is highly uneven, and several factors determine rainfall in different regions:\nRainfall generally decreases from the equator towards the poles.\nCoastal regions receive more rainfall than interiors of continents due to proximity to water bodies.\nRainfall is heavier over the oceans than landmasses.\nFrom 35\u00b0 to 40\u00b0 N and S, rainfall is higher on eastern coasts, while from 45\u00b0 to 65\u00b0 N and S, due to westerlies, it is higher on western margins.\nMountains parallel to coasts result in higher rainfall on windward slopes and less on leeward or rain-shadow sides.\nEquatorial areas, windward mountain slopes, and monsoon lands get heavy rainfall (>200 cm/year), interiors get moderate (100\u2013200 cm), and rain-shadow areas and high latitudes receive the least (<50 cm/year).\nRainfall distribution also varies seasonally; some regions receive rain all year, while others have marked dry and wet seasons."
